1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,357
Hi, I'm Elmor.

2
00:00:01,357 --> 00:00:11,137
This is a tour of the architecture behind Orders Shop: a .NET 10 API, an ASP.NET Core backend for frontend, and a React single page app.

3
00:00:11,137 --> 00:00:20,198
I built it with AI assisted development, and I'll show you how the decisions, the tests, and the reviews keep that speed safe.

4
00:00:21,148 --> 00:00:23,135
Here is the whole system.

5
00:00:23,135 --> 00:00:27,030
The browser talks to exactly one server, the BFF.

6
00:00:27,030 --> 00:00:29,812
All it holds is an HttpOnly cookie.

7
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:37,461
The BFF keeps the session in Valkey, and forwards allow listed calls to the Orders API.

8
00:00:37,461 --> 00:00:41,850
The API owns the business rules, and talks to PostgreSQL.

9
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:46,165
The whole stack starts with one docker compose command.

10
00:00:46,165 --> 00:00:49,592
And the API did not change when the front end was added.

11
00:00:50,542 --> 00:00:52,873
Before any code, there is a plan.

12
00:00:52,873 --> 00:00:57,960
Requirements, a threat model, and fifteen architecture decision records.

13
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:01,846
Each one compares options, and writes down why one won.

14
00:01:02,796 --> 00:01:06,889
The AI writes code quickly, so the guardrails are strict.

15
00:01:06,889 --> 00:01:08,254
Architecture tests.

16
00:01:08,254 --> 00:01:10,552
Banned APIs that fail the build.

17
00:01:10,552 --> 00:01:16,225
And a fresh context review by separate reviewer agents, before anything merges.

18
00:01:16,225 --> 00:01:19,600
The decisions are mine and the product owner's.

19
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:21,252
The AI does the typing.

20
00:01:22,202 --> 00:01:28,567
The API uses Clean Architecture for the projects, and vertical slices for the folders.

21
00:01:28,567 --> 00:01:35,450
Domain, Application, Infrastructure, and one API project, which is the only composition root.

22
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:42,702
Inside each project, the first folder is the business feature: Products, Customers, Orders.

23
00:01:42,702 --> 00:01:49,696
Features never reference each other, and architecture tests fail the build if a layer rule is broken.

24
00:01:50,646 --> 00:01:54,216
A request enters a thin FastEndpoints endpoint.

25
00:01:54,216 --> 00:01:55,507
Look at this one.

26
00:01:55,507 --> 00:02:02,190
A route, an explicit auth policy, an OpenAPI summary, and one line that sends a command.

27
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:08,776
The handler depends only on a messaging package, never on the web framework.

28
00:02:08,776 --> 00:02:15,375
It returns a Result, and one extension maps that to HTTP, with ProblemDetails for errors.

29
00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:19,676
Reads skip the bus, and use EF Core projections or Dapper.

30
00:02:20,626 --> 00:02:24,160
PostgreSQL eighteen is the single database.

31
00:02:24,160 --> 00:02:28,105
EF Core handles every write and every migration.

32
00:02:28,105 --> 00:02:34,762
Dapper handles the views and report functions, where hand written SQL beats LINQ.

33
00:02:35,712 --> 00:02:37,276
Rows are never deleted.

34
00:02:37,276 --> 00:02:45,708
Soft delete is enforced by an interceptor, and by the database itself, because the application role has no DELETE privilege.

35
00:02:45,708 --> 00:02:52,848
An audit interceptor writes the audit row in the same transaction, and never stores personal data values.

36
00:02:53,798 --> 00:02:55,637
Concurrency is explicit.

37
00:02:55,637 --> 00:03:01,615
Entity tags come from the PostgreSQL xmin column, so updates require If-Match.

38
00:03:01,615 --> 00:03:09,662
Orders lock stock rows with SELECT FOR UPDATE, always in product ID order, so deadlocks are designed out.

39
00:03:10,612 --> 00:03:13,701
Authentication is our own JWT issuer.

40
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:16,624
Access tokens last fifteen minutes.

41
00:03:16,624 --> 00:03:24,556
Refresh tokens are hashed, rotate on every use, and reusing one revokes the whole token family.

42
00:03:25,506 --> 00:03:30,043
Authorization is deny by default, with four named policies.

43
00:03:30,043 --> 00:03:36,349
A customer asking for someone else's data gets a 404, not a 403, so nothing leaks.

44
00:03:36,349 --> 00:03:40,578
Three rate limit policies protect the sensitive routes.

45
00:03:41,528 --> 00:03:48,277
Only products are cached, with HybridCache and Valkey, and a Valkey outage never causes an error.

46
00:03:48,277 --> 00:03:56,696
Each dependency has exactly one retry layer, the database has a circuit breaker, and OpenTelemetry traces the whole path.

47
00:03:57,646 --> 00:03:58,814
Now the BFF.

48
00:03:58,814 --> 00:04:01,246
I compared three options.

49
00:04:01,246 --> 00:04:03,484
Next.js as the backend.

50
00:04:03,484 --> 00:04:07,571
A plain single page app that holds tokens.

51
00:04:07,571 --> 00:04:10,198
Or a thin ASP.NET Core BFF.

52
00:04:11,148 --> 00:04:15,962
The BFF won for one reason: tokens never reach the browser.

53
00:04:15,962 --> 00:04:24,612
It also gives one origin, so no CORS, no Node server in production, and the same .NET pipeline as the API.

54
00:04:25,562 --> 00:04:34,257
On sign in, the BFF calls the API and stores the tokens in a Valkey session, encrypted with Data Protection.

55
00:04:34,257 --> 00:04:36,511
The browser gets one cookie.

56
00:04:36,511 --> 00:04:43,274
It is HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite Strict, and uses the double underscore Host prefix.

57
00:04:44,224 --> 00:04:49,793
So even if a script were injected into the page, there is no token for it to steal.

58
00:04:49,793 --> 00:04:53,752
Admin sessions also end after thirty minutes of inactivity.

59
00:04:54,702 --> 00:04:56,621
Here is the trickiest part.

60
00:04:56,621 --> 00:05:00,601
The API treats a second use of a refresh token as theft.

61
00:05:00,601 --> 00:05:09,486
If a page fires twenty requests while the token is expiring, a naive client triggers a refresh storm, and signs the user out.

62
00:05:10,436 --> 00:05:14,630
The BFF solves it with a single flight refresh coordinator.

63
00:05:14,630 --> 00:05:19,961
One refresh runs per session, and every other request awaits the same task.

64
00:05:19,961 --> 00:05:25,292
A test proves that twenty parallel requests cause exactly one refresh call.

65
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:29,852
The proxy is YARP with an explicit allow list.

66
00:05:29,852 --> 00:05:31,814
Unknown paths return 404.

67
00:05:31,814 --> 00:05:34,639
Login and refresh are never proxied.

68
00:05:34,639 --> 00:05:41,074
The BFF strips the browser's cookie, and adds the bearer token on the way through.

69
00:05:42,024 --> 00:05:48,249
Every state changing request needs a custom X-CSRF header and a matching Origin.

70
00:05:48,249 --> 00:05:59,376
A strict content security policy blocks inline scripts, rate limits apply per IP at the edge, and one trace ID flows from the BFF into the API.

71
00:06:00,326 --> 00:06:07,554
The front end is Vite and React nineteen with the React Compiler, so there is no manual memoization.

72
00:06:07,554 --> 00:06:12,542
TypeScript runs in strict mode, and React Router loads routes lazily.

73
00:06:13,492 --> 00:06:16,229
Server state lives in TanStack Query.

74
00:06:16,229 --> 00:06:18,818
Forms use React Hook Form with Zod.

75
00:06:18,818 --> 00:06:24,292
Styling is Tailwind four, with accessible Radix primitives through shadcn.

76
00:06:25,242 --> 00:06:31,224
Code is organized by feature, and lint rules stop one feature importing another's internals.

77
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:33,630
The Admin area is its own lazy chunk.

78
00:06:33,630 --> 00:06:42,018
The customer bundle is about one hundred sixty kilobytes gzipped, under a two hundred kilobyte budget that the pipeline enforces.

79
00:06:42,968 --> 00:06:49,332
API types are generated from the committed OpenAPI document, and the pipeline fails if they drift.

80
00:06:49,332 --> 00:06:55,112
So a backend change that breaks the front end is caught at build time, not in production.

81
00:06:56,062 --> 00:07:02,966
Errors arrive as ProblemDetails with a trace ID, and a slug maps to a plain message in the UI.

82
00:07:02,966 --> 00:07:09,430
Product edits send the entity tag back as If-Match, so the API can reject stale updates.

83
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:18,683
And here it is working: the catalog, an order placed, the account deletion flow, the admin dashboard, and product management.

84
00:07:18,683 --> 00:07:21,406
All against the real API, with demo data.

85
00:07:21,406 --> 00:07:25,524
There is a separate walkthrough video if you want every click.

86
00:07:26,474 --> 00:07:27,940
Quality is layered.

87
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:30,331
The BFF has ninety seven tests.

88
00:07:30,331 --> 00:07:38,509
The React app has two hundred thirty two, plus forty end to end Playwright tests with accessibility scans.

89
00:07:38,509 --> 00:07:46,610
The API has unit tests, integration tests on real PostgreSQL with Testcontainers, and architecture tests.

90
00:07:47,560 --> 00:07:58,896
Azure Pipelines enforces it: an API freeze check, type checks, lint with zero warnings, bundle budgets, dependency audits, license checks, and Docker builds.

91
00:07:58,896 --> 00:08:04,456
The security reviews of the BFF and the front end are written up in the repo.

92
00:08:05,406 --> 00:08:08,248
What does this mean for you as a client?

93
00:08:08,248 --> 00:08:11,729
AI assisted development gets features built fast.

94
00:08:11,729 --> 00:08:17,768
But every decision is documented, every rule is tested, and every change is reviewed.

95
00:08:17,768 --> 00:08:21,534
You get speed, without a codebase nobody understands.

96
00:08:22,484 --> 00:08:27,016
To be clear, this is a proof of concept, built to show the approach.

97
00:08:27,016 --> 00:08:32,015
It lives on a feature branch and will be merged to main after final review.

98
00:08:32,015 --> 00:08:36,280
The Admin screens for orders, reports, and audit logs come next.

99
00:08:36,280 --> 00:08:40,279
If you want this level of rigor on your product, let's talk.

100
00:08:40,279 --> 00:08:41,612
Thanks for watching.
